__registerContent("quickrecall/polity/anti-defection", {"notes":{"qr-anti-defection":{"era":"Quick Recall — Polity & Governance","significance":["The 52nd and 91st Constitutional Amendments and the Tenth Schedule are among the most tested polity facts in Prelims","The grounds for disqualification and the 'merger' exception are frequent match-the-following and statement-based questions","Kihoto Hollohan (1992) and the role/judicial review of the Presiding Officer's decision are recurring Mains and Prelims themes"],"features":[{"key":"Origin","main":"Introduced by the 52nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1985 (Rajiv Gandhi government), which added the TENTH SCHEDULE to the Constitution. Because it is part of the Constitution, it is a constitutional law.","note":"Popularly called the 'Anti-Defection Law'."},{"key":"Grounds for disqualification","main":"A member of a House is disqualified if:\n(a) a member of a political party voluntarily gives up membership of that party, or votes/abstains contrary to the party WHIP without prior permission, and the act is not condoned by the party within 15 days;\n(b) an INDEPENDENT member joins any political party after the election;\n(c) a NOMINATED member joins any political party after 6 months from taking their seat.","note":"Only these categories attract disqualification under the Schedule."},{"key":"91st Amendment Act, 2003","main":"Deleted the earlier 'SPLIT' exception (which had protected a split by one-third members). Now only a genuine MERGER survives. Also inserted Articles 75(1A)/164(1A) capping the Council of Ministers at 15% of the strength of the House (minimum 12 in states), and Articles 75(1B)/164(1B) barring a disqualified member from being appointed minister until re-elected or the term ends.","note":"Article 361B similarly bars disqualified members from remunerative political posts."},{"key":"The merger exception","main":"Defection is NOT treated as disqualification if at least TWO-THIRDS (2/3) of the members of a legislature party agree to a merger with another party. The earlier one-third 'split' protection was removed by the 91st Amendment.","note":"So splinter groups formed by 1/3 members are no longer protected."},{"key":"Deciding authority & judicial review","main":"The PRESIDING OFFICER (Speaker/Chairman) decides disqualification questions. Paragraph 7 of the Tenth Schedule barred courts from any jurisdiction. In Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachillhu (1992), the Supreme Court struck down Para 7 as unconstitutional (it needed ratification by states) and held that the Presiding Officer acts as a TRIBUNAL, so the decision is subject to JUDICIAL REVIEW by the High Courts and Supreme Court.","note":"Judicial review is limited but available on grounds of mala fides, perversity, etc."}],"traps":[{"label":"52nd vs 91st Amendment","lines":["52nd CAA (1985): introduced the law and the Tenth Schedule.","91st CAA (2003): removed the 1/3 SPLIT exception and capped the Council of Ministers at 15%.","Common trap: attributing the merger/split change to the wrong amendment."]},{"label":"Split vs Merger","lines":["Original law protected a SPLIT by 1/3 members — this is NO LONGER valid (deleted in 2003).","Only a MERGER agreed by 2/3 members is now protected."]},{"label":"Nominated vs Independent members","lines":["Independent member: disqualified if they join ANY party after election.","Nominated member: disqualified if they join a party after 6 MONTHS of taking their seat (they may join within 6 months)."]},{"label":"Whip and disqualification","lines":["Violating the party whip attracts disqualification only when not condoned by the party within 15 days.","Courts have observed the whip is decisive mainly in matters like money bills, confidence/no-confidence motions — not every vote."]}],"mainsAngle":"The Anti-Defection Law sought to curb the 'Aaya Ram Gaya Ram' culture of unprincipled floor-crossing, but by binding legislators to the party whip it has arguably weakened intra-party dissent, debate and the accountability of the executive to the legislature. Reform proposals — restricting the whip to confidence motions and money bills, and shifting the adjudicatory role from the Speaker to an independent tribunal or the Election Commission — seek to balance stability with legislative freedom.","mainsTheme":"Anti-defection — party discipline versus legislative independence","recall":["52nd CAA 1985 → introduced law, added Tenth Schedule","91st CAA 2003 → deleted 1/3 split; capped CoM at 15% of House","Merger exception: 2/3 members must agree","Deciding authority: Presiding Officer (Speaker/Chairman)","Kihoto Hollohan (1992): struck down Para 7; decision subject to judicial review","Nominated member: 6-month window to join a party"]}},"quizzes":{"qr-anti-defection":{"title":"Anti-Defection Law","questions":[{"q":"The Anti-Defection Law was added to the Constitution by which amendment?","options":["42nd Amendment Act, 1976","44th Amendment Act, 1978","52nd Amendment Act, 1985","61st Amendment Act, 1989"],"correct":2,"explain":"The 52nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1985 introduced the Anti-Defection Law and added the Tenth Schedule to the Constitution."},{"q":"The Anti-Defection Law is contained in which Schedule of the Constitution?","options":["Ninth Schedule","Tenth Schedule","Eleventh Schedule","Twelfth Schedule"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Anti-Defection provisions are in the Tenth Schedule, added by the 52nd Amendment in 1985."},{"q":"What key change did the 91st Constitutional Amendment Act, 2003 make to the Anti-Defection Law?","options":["It introduced the law for the first time","It deleted the 'split' (one-third) exception","It abolished the role of the Speaker","It extended the law to local bodies"],"correct":1,"explain":"The 91st Amendment deleted the one-third 'split' exception; now only a merger agreed by two-thirds of members is protected. It also capped the Council of Ministers at 15% of the House."},{"q":"Under the current law, defection is NOT treated as disqualification if a merger is agreed to by at least:","options":["One-third of the members","One-half of the members","Two-thirds of the members","Three-fourths of the members"],"correct":2,"explain":"After the 91st Amendment, only a merger agreed to by at least two-thirds of the legislature party's members is exempt from disqualification."},{"q":"In which case did the Supreme Court hold that the Presiding Officer's decision under the Tenth Schedule is subject to judicial review?","options":["Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (1973)","Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachillhu (1992)","S. R. Bommai v. Union of India (1994)","Indra Sawhney v. Union of India (1992)"],"correct":1,"explain":"In Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachillhu (1992), the Supreme Court struck down Paragraph 7 (which barred courts) and held that the Presiding Officer acts as a tribunal, making the decision subject to judicial review."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about disqualification under the Anti-Defection Law:","I. An independent member is disqualified if they join any political party after the election.","II. A nominated member is disqualified if they join a political party after six months of taking their seat.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct. Independents are disqualified for joining any party post-election; nominated members get a six-month window to join a party without disqualification."},{"q":"Who is the deciding authority on questions of disqualification under the Tenth Schedule?","options":["The President / Governor","The Election Commission of India","The Presiding Officer (Speaker / Chairman) of the House","The Supreme Court directly"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Presiding Officer of the House decides disqualification questions, though the decision is subject to judicial review as held in Kihoto Hollohan (1992)."},{"q":"The 91st Constitutional Amendment Act, 2003 capped the total number of ministers in the Council of Ministers at what proportion of the strength of the House?","options":["10%","12%","15%","20%"],"correct":2,"explain":"The 91st Amendment inserted Articles 75(1A)/164(1A) capping the Council of Ministers at 15% of the total strength of the House (with a minimum of 12 in states)."},{"q":"A nominated member of a House incurs disqualification under the Tenth Schedule if he joins a political party after the expiry of:","options":["One month from taking his seat","Three months from taking his seat","Six months from taking his seat","One year from taking his seat"],"correct":2,"explain":"A nominated member is disqualified if he joins any political party after six months from taking his seat. He may join a party within that six-month window."},{"q":["Consider the following statements regarding the 91st Amendment Act, 2003:","I. It deleted the earlier 'split' exception based on one-third members.","II. It barred a disqualified member from being appointed a minister until re-election or the end of the term.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct. The 91st Amendment removed the one-third split exception and, through Articles 75(1B)/164(1B), barred disqualified members from being appointed ministers until re-elected or the term ends."},{"q":"Which Article, inserted by the 91st Amendment, bars a disqualified member from holding a remunerative political post?","options":["Article 361A","Article 361B","Article 164(1A)","Article 75(1B)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Article 361B bars a member disqualified on the ground of defection from being appointed to any remunerative political post until re-election or the end of the term."},{"q":"In Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachillhu (1992), which paragraph of the Tenth Schedule did the Supreme Court strike down as unconstitutional?","options":["Paragraph 2","Paragraph 4","Paragraph 6","Paragraph 7"],"correct":3,"explain":"The Court struck down Paragraph 7, which had barred the jurisdiction of courts, holding it required ratification by states; the Presiding Officer's decision was thus made subject to judicial review."},{"q":"The phrase 'Aaya Ram Gaya Ram', which the Anti-Defection Law sought to curb, refers to:","options":["Corruption in the bureaucracy","Unprincipled floor-crossing / frequent party switching by legislators","Delay in passing bills","Misuse of the whip in money bills"],"correct":1,"explain":"'Aaya Ram Gaya Ram' symbolises the culture of unprincipled and frequent floor-crossing by legislators, which the Anti-Defection Law aimed to end."},{"q":"A member of a political party can be disqualified for voting or abstaining contrary to the party whip only if the act is not condoned by the party within:","options":["7 days","10 days","15 days","30 days"],"correct":2,"explain":"Violating the party whip attracts disqualification only if the party does not condone the act within 15 days."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about the merger exception under the Tenth Schedule:","I. A merger is deemed to have taken place only if at least two-thirds of the members of the legislature party agree to it.","II. Members who accept such a merger are not disqualified.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct. A protected merger requires the agreement of at least two-thirds of the legislature party's members, and those who accept it are not disqualified."},{"q":"The minimum size of the Council of Ministers in a state, as fixed by the 91st Amendment, is:","options":["10 ministers","12 ministers","15 ministers","20 ministers"],"correct":1,"explain":"The 91st Amendment fixed a minimum of 12 ministers for a state Council of Ministers, even where 15% of the House strength would be fewer."},{"q":"Match List-I (Amendment) with List-II (Contribution) and select the correct answer:\nA. 52nd Amendment  B. 91st Amendment\n1. Deleted the split exception  2. Added the Tenth Schedule","options":["A-2, B-1","A-1, B-2","A-2, B-2","A-1, B-1"],"correct":0,"explain":"The 52nd Amendment (1985) added the Tenth Schedule; the 91st Amendment (2003) deleted the one-third split exception. Hence A-2, B-1."},{"q":"In Kihoto Hollohan (1992), the Supreme Court held that while deciding disqualification, the Presiding Officer acts as a:","options":["Purely political authority","Tribunal whose decision is subject to judicial review","Final authority immune from any review","Delegate of the Election Commission"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Court held that the Presiding Officer functions as a tribunal, so the decision is amenable to judicial review on grounds such as mala fides and perversity."},{"q":["Consider the following statements:","I. The Anti-Defection Law applies to both Parliament and State Legislatures.","II. The whip is generally decisive in matters such as money bills and confidence/no-confidence motions.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct. The Tenth Schedule applies to members of both Parliament and State Legislatures, and courts have observed the whip is decisive mainly in crucial votes like money bills and confidence motions."},{"q":"Which body has been proposed by reformers as an alternative to the Presiding Officer for deciding defection cases?","options":["The Council of Ministers","An independent tribunal or the Election Commission","The Attorney General","The Comptroller and Auditor General"],"correct":1,"explain":"Reform proposals suggest shifting the adjudicatory role from the Speaker to an independent tribunal or the Election Commission to ensure impartiality."}]}}});
